   $q$Which choice lacks a main verb and is therefore a fragment?$q$, $q$[{"text":"The volunteers who spent the summer recording bird calls along the marsh trail shared their data.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"long-subject-misread"},{"text":"The volunteers recorded bird calls all summer; the marsh trail was their busiest site.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"semicolon-misread"},{"text":"Recording bird calls along the marsh trail took the volunteers the entire summer.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"gerund-subject-misread-as-fragment"},{"text":"The volunteers who spent the summer recording bird calls along the marsh trail and the river levee.","is_correct":true}]$q$::jsonb, $q$The volunteers who spent the summer recording bird calls along the marsh trail and the river levee.$q$, $q$In the choice ending "and the river levee," the only verb, "spent," belongs to the "who" clause, so the subject "volunteers" has no main verb. The choice beginning "Recording bird calls" is complete because the gerund phrase is the subject of "took."$q$, 'draft', null)
