-- Passage wg12-writing-with-evidence-p02
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('d95e0f64-caa6-5efb-83c0-0c458f6a3d65', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p02', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: The Wagon That Listened$q$, $q$(1) Historian Hyun-woo Baek's essay "Shelves on Wheels" examines a horse-drawn book wagon that carried library books to farms in Larch County from 1905 to 1921. (2) Baek argues that the wagon succeeded not simply because it brought books closer to farm families but because its librarian treated each stop as a conversation about what readers wanted. (3) The librarian, Odile Fairweather, kept a notebook of requests at every farm and returned two weeks later with the titles people had asked for. (4) She also left pamphlets on soil, canning and bookkeeping for parents, along with storybooks for younger children. (5) Baek notes that a state-run book wagon in a neighboring county, which carried a fixed set of titles chosen by an office in the capital, stopped running after only three years.

(6) The loan records support Baek's reading. (7) In the wagon's first year, Fairweather lent about 600 books, but by 1912 she was lending more than 4,000 books a year. (8) ____ (9) Baek also quotes a farmer's letter thanking Fairweather for "remembering that my boy wanted the book about bridges." (10) The wagon was painted dark green and pulled by two horses named Pepper and Sage. (11) These details suggest that families kept borrowing because the wagon answered their own questions.

(12) Overall, the book wagon was an important part of history.$q$, 'draft')
