   $q$Which revision of sentence 7 best explains how the evidence in sentences 5 and 6 supports Haddad's claim?$q$, $q$[{"text":"Because the visual report missed the cracked bearing and the vibration data pointed straight to it, the sensors sent inspectors to damage they had overlooked.","is_correct":true},{"text":"In 2022, the sensors noticed that one joint vibrated more on cold mornings, and a team went out to look at it.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"restates-evidence"},{"text":"This example shows that cold weather is the main cause of damage to steel bridges in most parts of the country.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"unsupported-inference"},{"text":"This example proves that visual inspections are useless and that engineers should stop climbing bridges altogether.","is_correct":false,"misconception_token":"contradicts-source"}]$q$::jsonb, $q$Because the visual report missed the cracked bearing and the vibration data pointed straight to it, the sensors sent inspectors to damage they had overlooked.$q$, $q$The correct revision explains why the example matters: the data pointed inspectors to damage the eye had missed, which is Haddad's "where to look" claim. The "useless" version contradicts sentence 4, where Haddad says sensors do not replace inspectors.$q$, 'draft', '90aee6ac-600b-53b0-917e-2e2927ad6d83')
