-- Passage wg12-writing-with-evidence-p05
insert into public.passages (id, code, skill_id, sat_alignment, grade_band, title, body, status)
values ('8fa8f357-fefe-5276-8bda-5e42f95a881d', 'wg12-writing-with-evidence-p05', (select id from public.skills where slug = 'write-g12-writing-with-evidence'), null,
        '9-12', $q$Draft: Slow Starts, Strong Finishes$q$, $q$(1) In "Running Together," exercise researcher Bongani Mthembu studies what happened after the Harlow City Marathon introduced pace groups in 2018. (2) A pace group is a cluster of runners who follow an experienced volunteer carrying a sign with a target finishing time. (3) Mthembu argues that the groups help first-time marathoners finish mainly by keeping them from starting too fast. (4) Before 2018, about 81 percent of first-time entrants finished the race. (5) In the four years after pace groups began, that figure rose to 89 percent. (6) Mthembu's split times show why: runners in pace groups ran the first ten miles an average of four minutes slower than first-timers running alone, yet they finished an average of nine minutes faster.

(7) In other words, the runners who held back early had more energy left for the final miles, where most dropouts happen. (8) Some readers might suspect that the higher finishing rate came from better weather instead. (9) ____ (10) The race course passes a historic train station and crosses the river twice. (11) Taken together, Mthembu's numbers show that pace groups make running fun for everyone.$q$, 'draft')
